                          "String": "D(+)-Glucose is formed in plants by photosynthesis(1). D(+)-Glucose is a main source of energy for living organisms and occurs naturally and in the free state in fruits and other parts of plants(2). Normal human blood contains 0.08-0.1% D(+)-glucose(1,2). Glucose predominantly occurs in nature in the form of the D-enantiomer(3). D(+)-Glucose can be considered the parent compound of the monosaccharide family, because it is the most abundant monosaccharide in nature. It occurs as such in many fruits and plants and constitutes the basic building unit of starch, cellulose, and glycogen(4).",
